<ul></ul>

<%= header(2, 'Marking the last cat to detect consecutive duplicated rare cats between events') %>
<ul></ul>

<%= header(2, 'Switching track is the effect of making a half roll') %>
<ul>
  <li>
    For each regular rolls, or a roll of cat, the game will roll the
    dice (seed) twice, once for deciding the rarity, and once for
    deciding which cat for that rarity.
  </li>
  <li>
    The game will only roll the dice (seed) once when it's rolling a
    guaranteed uber, or when it's replacing your duplicated rare cat for
    another rare cat.
  </li>
  <li>
    In summary, this means:
    <ul>
      <li>A roll of cat = a roll of rarity + a roll of which cat (2 rolls of the seed)</li>
      <li>A roll of guaranteed = a roll of which cat (1 roll of the seed)</li>
      <li>A roll of replacing a dupe rare cat = a roll of another rare cat (1 roll of the seed)</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>With this in mind, we can also say that a guaranteed roll is a half roll (of cat).</li>
  <li>
    We switch to the other track when the game makes a half roll
    (or an odd number of half rolls when there are
    <a href="#increased-rates-with-repeated-cats">increased rates with repeated cats</a>).
  </li>
  <li>Let's look at examples for both tracks A and B.</li>
  <li>
    For track A, we jump from 1A to 11B (1 + 10.5 = 11.5), and given that track B is 0.5 roll ahead,
    11.5A = 11B.
  </li>
  <li>
    Here's an example switching from B to A, and for the same offset that 1B
    is actually 1.5A, we jump from 1B to <strong>12A</strong>
    (1.5 + 10.5 = 12).
  </li>
</ul>

<%= erb :table, cats: arg[:help].pick(arg[:help].pick(arg[:help].guaranteed_tracks, 1, 0, true), 1, 1, true) %>

<%= header(2, 'Marking the next position') %>
<ul>
  <li>
    When we want to plan a few sequence of guaranteed rolls, to avoid
    confusion, we likely do not want to advance the tracks just yet.
  </li>
  <li>
    Marking the next position can come handy in this case. We can click on
    the label of the position, for example 11B, to mark that as the next
    position.
  </li>
  <li>
    Highlighting following that, will start from 11B rather than 1A in that
    case. This should help us visualize the paths better with multiple
    tabs opened for each of the guaranteed rolls.
  </li>
</ul>

